.TH smi-new-version "1" "November 2026" "SMI Toolset 0.156.0" "SMI Project Command"
.SH NAME
smi-new-version \- change the version markers of a project to a new version
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-new-version
.I NEW_VERSION
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The \fBsmi-new-version\fR command is run in the root folder of a project. \fINEW_VERSION\fR is a semantic
version: \fIMAJOR\fB.\fIMINOR\fB.\fIPATCH\fR, numbers without leading zeros, optionally followed by a
\fB-\fIpre-release\fR and a \fB+\fIbuild\fR part, such as \fB1.2.0\fR or \fB1.2.0-rc.1\fR. Without exactly one
argument, or with one that is no semantic version, it prints the usage and exits with status 1.
.PP
The project says which files hold its version in the file \fB.new-version\fR of that folder, a shell file
that the command includes. The command then calls its function \fBdoVersionChange\fR with \fINEW_VERSION\fR.
It stops on the first failing command.
.SH "THE .new-version FILE"
.PP
\fBdoVersionChange\fR gets the new version as \fB$1\fR and changes every version marker of the project. The
helper functions of the library \fBproject.sh\fR are available, described in \fBsmi-project-cleanup\fR(1),
for example \fBprojectEditFile\fR \fIFILE\fR \fBsed -e\fR \fIEXPRESSION\fR, which edits a file in place with
any sed and leaves a missing file alone.
.PP
The \fB.new-version\fR of the project root can also only include another file, so the actual one lives
beside the build scripts, as in setmy-info-scripts:
.PP
.nf
.RS
\&. ./src/main/sh/build/.new-version
.RE
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
A .new-version that changes the version of a Makefile:
.PP
.nf
.RS
doVersionChange() {
    projectEditFile Makefile sed -e "s/^VERSION = .*/VERSION = $1/"
}
.RE
.fi
.PP
.B smi-new-version 0.157.0
.SH AUTHOR
.PP
Command written by Imre Tabur.
